{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1117546-let-s-talk-art","id":1117546,"title":"Let's talk art","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-07-17T19:40:38-07:00","id":1117546,"title":"Let's talk art","content":"What is your favorite book in terms of art? Any specific pieces? Favorite artists?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12561-shawarbaaz","name":"Shawarbaaz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12561_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"467"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-17T20:28:53-07:00","id":1117570,"title":"","content":"Honestly? Blood &amp; Smoke. And I say B&amp;S over Requiem 2e because I liked the former's cover better, but both are gorgeous books that made the setting of the game fully vibrant for me in a way 1e never really did.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://tinyurl.com/2p8ss75f\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Chris H</b></a>/<b>EndDaysEngine</b> | <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/EndDaysEngine\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Patreon</b></a></span>| <b>He/His </b>| <b>Currently Writing</b>: <i>[Redacted], [Redacted]</i><br /> <br /> <b>CofD booklists: </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/DRdB7h\" target=\"_blank\">Beast</a> I <a href=\"https://goo.gl/x1FPpM\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/ZSCx5U\" target=\"_blank\">Demon</a> | <a href=\"http://shorturl.at/syDK9\" target=\"_blank\">Deviant</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/Mh5M6m\" target=\"_blank\">Geist</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/gSjfm5\" target=\"_blank\">Hunter</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/qj5aX9\" target=\"_blank\">Mage</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/2HCpjg\" target=\"_blank\">Mummy</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/68yRHY\" target=\"_blank\">Promethean</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/a4mjqA\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/hrULtN\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4433-second-chances","name":"Second Chances","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4202"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T00:02:53-07:00","id":1117627,"title":"","content":"Wicked Dead and Immortal Sinners had some good art.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12924-orzhov","name":"Orzhov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"74"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T05:56:26-07:00","id":1117690,"title":"","content":"I'll probably second Immortal sinners. Art in an RPG book is a tricky thing. It can be cheesy, ugly or very competently done (colorful, nice composition, good anatomy), but to me, it's never quite necessary. It's always purely illustrative and usually a bit gratuitous, simply a means to an end to make the book look nice and maybe suggest some atmosphere through the visuals. It's like the sort stories or flavour text you find here and there (mostly those that are between chapters, totally separated from the more informative parts). They can be written relatively well, but they're purely and accessory to give some inspiration to GMs and players. A GM or player who already know what they want will just skip them to focus on the parts of he book that lets them know how the game is played on a basic level. To me, they show an interesting attempt from the game creators to create a form of smooth entity that is the RPG book, but I've never seen it result in something that's more than the sum of its parts, and I'd like to, because as an object, an RPG book is an interesting thing. How do you classify it ? Can you call it literature ?<br /> <br /> In a way, while there was so much text that didn't really have much consequence in the end that I wouldn't read them whole, the clanbooks had an interesting idea to make the information book about the clan a fictional investigation about the subject matter with various sources and stuff. It worked well with the intent to have Requiem have a vague historical background. That was more for the text, though, the illustrations didn't really bring much, although the look of the pages (with post-its, torn pages and so) again worked towards building something coherent and a bit unique.<br /> <br /> Immortal Sinners and, to an extent, Wicked Dead's art, don't really answer that problem for me, but since the books talked about specific things, and specific characters, I thought it was nice to have a visual reference to what they looked like. It's also potentially a good gaming help since I sometimes like to make portraits of characters rather than describe them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7104-s-brechen","name":"S. Brechen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"171"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T06:21:17-07:00","id":1117698,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>S. Brechen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1117546-let-s-talk-art?p=1117690#post1117690\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's always purely illustrative and usually a bit gratuitous, simply a means to an end to make the book look nice and maybe suggest some atmosphere through the visuals.</div> </div> </div> </div>i have to disagree to some extent, good art and flavor text make or break the book for me, I'd never read an RPG book that is simply explaining the rules without getting me interested enough to play the game. If I open up a drivethru preview and find the book to be made up of half-photoshop slathered pictures and half poor writing there's no way I'm going to buy it. No matter what interesting additions to the mechanics it contain.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12561-shawarbaaz","name":"Shawarbaaz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12561_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"467"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T12:10:37-07:00","id":1117876,"title":"","content":"Anything by Abrar Abjal is fucking amazing IMHP.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T13:44:14-07:00","id":1117915,"title":"","content":"Actually, it's not one picture in particular, but i find it kind of interesting to see various artists' renditions (or different versions by the same artist, not really sure) of the same characters, like Birch who's been drawn at least 4 different times in the books I believe, and with variations each time. Kind of makes me wish there were a bit more of an emphasis on &quot;official&quot; characters just so they would get different versions of themselves in the books.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shawarbaaz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1117546-let-s-talk-art?p=1117698#post1117698\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> i have to disagree to some extent, good art and flavor text make or break the book for me, I'd never read an RPG book that is simply explaining the rules without getting me interested enough to play the game. If I open up a drivethru preview and find the book to be made up of half-photoshop slathered pictures and half poor writing there's no way I'm going to buy it. No matter what interesting additions to the mechanics it contain.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're right, and I'm not suggesting a book like Requiem 2E should be condensed into 50 pages of pure rules and brief world descriptions. Nice layouts, pretty pictures and some flavour texts are appreciable and help digest the whole thing (although WoD/CoD flavour texts feel a bit too in love with themselves relatively often to me). That said, I can't really say they're more than that. They help digest what would otherwise be a boring and dry as fuck read by appealing the reader and breaking down big infodumps in smaller, more understandable portions, but theoretically, you could go through those condensed 50 pages if you really wanted to and the game you would run afterwards could be relatively similar. You could be influenced by a certain visual or a text, but that's about it. It is an important accessory, yes, as it helps convey the way the writers intended the game to be played (and the way that way can be disregarded), but I can't help but think this is a relatively awkward way to do it.<br /> <br /> But that's understandable. RPG books, to me seem to derive primarily from board games leaflets, which is to say, user manuals. The primary goal is to let the players know how the game works so that they may play by themselves. The nuance is that there is no board of props. When buying a board game, you buy all those things that let you play. In order to play Requiem, all you need is the manual. It's what people will see in stores, so it has to look fancy and appealing and not be made of cheap sheets of paper stapled together. So you add art because we nerds love art, so on a page talking about a dragon, you'll ask someone to draw a random dragon, and chances are you could look on deviantart and find another beautifully drawn dragon that would fit just as well. Texts are trickier because it has to be a bit more accurate to the setting. But both those things are somewhat superficial decorating. They do the job well, but they could be replaced by other similar texts and visuals and little would be lost.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7104,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7104-s-brechen","user":"S. Brechen","date":". 07-18-2017, 04:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7104-s-brechen","name":"S. Brechen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"171"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}